<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Minnesota</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Minnesota is a state in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midwestern-united-states">Midwest</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and Lake Superior, with its capital at Saint Paul. It holds the headwaters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> and was the homeland of the Dakota and later also of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ojibwe">Ojibwe</a>. American settlement grew after treaties in the 1850s, it became a state in 1858, and the Dakota War of 1862 led to the expulsion of most Dakota from the state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minnesota.jpg" alt="Minnesota" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Minnesota State Emblems Redesign Commission Vector: Jamie Eilat, Undertiago · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Minnesota.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Minnesota is the northernmost contiguous state, since the isolated Northwest Angle in Lake of the Woods County is the only part of the 48 contiguous states north of the 49th parallel. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manitoba">Manitoba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> to the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a> to the east, Iowa to the south and North Dakota and South Dakota to the west. It is known as the Land of 10,000 Lakes, with 14,420 bodies of fresh water of at least ten acres each. About a third of the state is forested, and much of the rest is prairie and farmland.</p><p>The name comes from the Dakota name for the Minnesota River, either mní sóta, clear blue water, or Mníssota, cloudy water. The state seal carries the phrase Mni Sóta Makoce, the land where the water reflects the skies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Native Americans have lived in Minnesota since the Woodland period of the 11th century BCE. The Upper Mississippian culture, made up of the Oneota and other Siouan speakers, emerged around 1000 CE and lasted until Europeans arrived in the 17th century. The Dakota lived in what is now Minnesota when French voyageurs and fur traders arrived, using the Grand Portage to reach trapping areas. The Anishinaabe, also called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ojibwe">Ojibwe</a> or Chippewa, were migrating in and formed an alliance with the Dakota. European powers destabilised the region, the alliance collapsed, and the Dakota-Ojibwe War followed.</p><p>The region was part of Spanish Louisiana from 1762 to 1802. The part east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>, and the part to the west came with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a> of 1803. The Territory of Minnesota was formed in 1849, with Saint Paul as its capital, and Minnesota was admitted as the 32nd state in 1858. Its motto, L'Étoile du Nord, The Star of the North, is the only state motto in French.</p><p>Settlers came from across the country, and growth was based first on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, agriculture and railroad construction. Into the early 20th century European immigrants arrived in large numbers, particularly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>. The state was at the forefront of labour rights, women's suffrage and political reform.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Saint Paul is the capital and Minneapolis the most populous city, and the two form the Twin Cities, where more than half the state's people live. Minnesota has 11 federally recognised Native American reservations, seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ojibwe">Ojibwe</a> and four Dakota, and its culture reflects Scandinavian and German influence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Minnesota” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the late 16th and early 17th centuries Dakota and Lakota speakers lived in the upper Mississippi region, in what is now <b>Minnesota</b> and Wisconsin, until Cree war parties armed with iron weapons pushed them west onto the Great Plains.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lakota-people">Lakota people · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>AIM’s Wounded Knee leaders were tried in a <b>Minnesota</b> federal court, which dismissed the case for governmental prosecutorial misconduct.</blockquote><a href="/movement/american-indian-movement">American Indian Movement · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>Around St. Cloud, <b>Minnesota</b>, settled in the 1850s by Catholic immigrants invited by the missionary Fr.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ich-hatt-einen-kameraden">Ich hatt' einen Kameraden (The Good Comrade)</a></li><li><blockquote>The valley runs south into the United States, where the river forms the border between <b>Minnesota</b> and North Dakota, and where exactly the song began is unresolved; but the weight of the evidence shows that it is this northern Red River the song means, not a southern or…</blockquote><a href="/songs/red-river-valley">Red River Valley</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Minnesota</b> Territory had been formed in 1849, and its founding population was so largely of New England origin that the state was dubbed &quot;the New England of the West&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-11">11 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The seven divisions of the Oceti Sakowin, the four Dakota peoples, the Yankton, the Yanktonai and the Lakota or Teton, originally lived around the upper Mississippi in what is now <b>Minnesota</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/great-sioux-nation">Great Sioux Nation</a></li></ul></section>
